  "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}+u-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $u=0.$",
